        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 力气",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "力气 — lìqi — \"physical strength\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "他的力气比我大 tā de lìqi bǐ wǒ dà — He's stronger than I am."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 力 (lì) — two strokes: a stroke that runs right, turns down and hooks to the left, and a long stroke falling to the left through it. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "力气 (lìqi) is built from 力 (lì) 'strength' and 气 'breath', both inherited from Middle Chinese, lik and khj+jH (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the first 'sinew' and the second 'breath'; the second is said light."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
